Tune the render LRU caches once #2233 has run on prod for a day (it adds `render_cache_*` counters and `[SLOW-FILL]` lines to the [STATS] logs). Keep the config identical on blue, green and yellow, sized so blue's 750MB heap is safe (worst case was ~620MB on 27 Oct; aim for 300-400MB shared). Pull the counters from all three containers (ask before each prod command) and per cache check hit rate, evictions, fill ms (`fillMs`/`fills`, `fillMaxMs`), `uncachedFills` and which blogs the `[SLOW-FILL]` lines name. Also check `redis_tracking_total_keys`: squeezed to 10k on 27 Oct, if it stays low keep client-side caching, if it refills toward 1M drop it (see the client-side caching entry below). Then investigate an admission rule: only store a fill in the cache if it took longer than a threshold (~5ms, probably per cache; `fullView` and `getAllCached` are far slower than the rest, which were mostly ~0ms). Cheap fills would stop evicting the expensive ones. Things to settle: the threshold must beat the cost of a hit (every hit does a `cloneDeep`); cost times frequency may matter more than cost alone; fill time includes event-loop delay, so smooth it or add hysteresis; keep coalescing concurrent fills (store then delete, or an own in-flight map as `getAllCached` has); a nashp-sized catalog is slow but huge and may not fit `maxSize`. A two-tier cache or GDSF-style cost-aware eviction are heavier alternatives. Once the capacity counters (missGhost/missCold, evictIdle, hitAge, uncachedBy, cloneMs) have run a day or two on prod, size each cache from them: a high missGhost share means more room would help, a mostly-cold one means it would not.

