Hunt down why a few updown.io probes per day fail outright (check `wplo`; 4 in 48h from Miami, Tokyo, Cape Town and Singapore, hours apart, never two at once). Evidence so far: each failure has no access-log line for that minute (no 499, 4xx or 5xx either), the probes' mean connection time is ~4s on three of them (consistent with a dropped handshake ACK plus the 1s/3s SYN retries) and the fourth connected fine then stalled waiting for the response; nginx's error log has nothing but routine noise at those times and the host was idle. Suspect: the :80/:443 accept queue overflowing (nginx's default backlog is 511 and `reuseport` gives each worker its own queue; the kernel's `ListenOverflows` counter is nonzero since boot, but bursty: 0 in the first 4 hours the sampler ran, so it can't yet be tied to a failure). #2228 sets `backlog=4096`; after it deploys, success = no failed samples for a few days. If they continue while `ListenOverflows` stays flat, the loss is on the network path (or a SYN drop upstream of the host, e.g. AWS security group or conntrack, though conntrack is nowhere near full) and not fixable here.

To check: updown.io check `wplo`, 48h Apdex heatmap, hover cells for "N failed", and the check's "Last results" list for per-second timestamps. On the host (`ssh blot`), `/tmp/listen-overflow.log` is a temporary sampler that logs UTC-timestamped `ListenOverflows` / `ListenDrops` / `TCPReqQFullDoCookies` and :443 accept-queue depth whenever a counter changes (plus a heartbeat every 10 minutes). For each jump, pull the access log for that same second (burst from one IP/host? gap?) and see whether it lines up with a failed probe. The counters can also be read directly: `awk '/^TcpExt:/{if(!h){split($0,k);h=1}else{split($0,v);for(i=2;i<=length(k);i++)if(k[i]~/^(ListenOverflows|ListenDrops|TCPReqQFullDoCookies)$/)print k[i],v[i]}}' /proc/net/netstat`. Uninstall the sampler when done: `ssh blot 'kill $(cat /tmp/overflow-sampler.pid); rm -f /tmp/overflow-sampler.sh /tmp/overflow-sampler.pid /tmp/listen-overflow.log'` (a detached `nohup sh` loop, not a service, so nothing else to disable; it does not survive a host reboot).

