Turn the airlock into a sandbox: run Node inside it so the whole "fetch or receive untrusted bytes -> decode/parse -> safe output" pipeline happens in a container with no secrets, no data mount and kernel-filtered egress, and the app only ever gets back a normalised result. Today the airlock only constrains where a fetch connects; the app containers (which hold every secret in /etc/blot/secrets.env and mount all blog folders read-write) still decompress (node-fetch gzip/br), sharp/libvips/libheif-decode, exiftool, pandoc, yauzl-unzip and cheerio-parse the hostile bytes - CVE-2026-111497 was reachable this way. Shape: rename airlock -> sandbox; add a Node worker under its own uid (own egress.nft rules, loopback still confined) exposing a small internal API that returns only simple re-encoded outputs - e.g. screenshot(url) -> re-encoded PNG/WebP, asset(url) -> metadata-stripped resized image variants, json(url) -> size-capped validated JSON, verify(domain) -> {status, match}; read-only rootfs, tmpfs scratch, per-job time/size limits. Phase it: (1) remote assets in posts (helper/transformer/download + the sharp resize/thumbnail after it), (2) bookmark screenshots (re-encode the PNG instead of committing Chromium's bytes), (3) importer downloads, (4) the bigger surface - converting synced blog-folder files (img/HEIC + exiftool, docx/odt/rtf via pandoc, gdoc/zip, template zip and image uploads) by posting bytes to a no-egress convert endpoint. Watch: the 512m memory budget and throughput (one container today), latency of shipping bytes both ways, and keeping the output formats boring enough that the app's own decode of them is low-risk. Also fix config/airlock/README.md, which still says the importer downloaders aren't routed (they are) and links a "Known sinks not covered" section that doesn't exist.
- something like NsJail or bubblewrap instead?

